# /demo was the scenario page. It is now a section of the homepage, so the
# target carries a fragment: Cloudflare's redirects support them in the
# destination ("Can include fragments, query strings, splats, and placeholders"),
# and only a line-leading # is a comment. The sitemap published /demo/ too.
#
# The cost, recorded because it is a real one: a destination fragment replaces
# whatever fragment the reader arrived with, so /demo#flip-a-byte lands on the
# section rather than on that scenario. Dropping the fragment here would invert
# the trade — deep links would keep working and every bare /demo would land at
# the top of the homepage, two bands above the thing it asked for. Bare /demo is
# what the sitemap, the old nav item, and any bookmark all point at.
/demo /#when-something-goes-wrong 308
/demo/ /#when-something-goes-wrong 308
